<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bab al-Futuh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bab al-Futuh is one of the monumental gates in the northern wall of Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, built in the late eleventh century under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-jamali">Badr al-Jamali</a>. Constructed of stone with rounded towers, it is a fine example of Fatimid military architecture. It survives today along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bab-al-nasr">Bab al-Nasr</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bab-zuwayla">Bab Zuwayla</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bab-al-futuh.jpg" alt="Bab al-Futuh" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cairo,_porte_settentrionali,_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>When the Fatimid general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawhar-al-siqilli">Jawhar</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 969 on behalf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Mu'izz, the city was enclosed by brick walls pierced by several gates. During the reign of Caliph al-Mustansir the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and army commander Badr al-Gamali rebuilt the walls and gates in stone, and the present gate was completed in 1087, together with the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bab-al-nasr">Bab al-Nasr</a>. It had first been called Bab al-Iqbal, the Gate of Prosperity, and took its present name, meaning Conquest Gate, when Badr rebuilt it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The gate stands at the northern end of al-Mu'izz Street. It is 22 metres high and 23 metres wide, solid stone in its lower two-thirds and rubble faced with dressed stone above, and its entrance is flanked by two tall round towers. Its decoration is richer and finer than that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bab-al-nasr">Bab al-Nasr</a>, and its stonework suggests northern Syrian or Byzantine traditions or craftsmen. Over the entrance a splayed arch is carved with lozenges holding rosette and cross motifs. Above it an overhang between the towers, a forerunner of the machicoulis, rests on brackets, two carved as rams' heads, the sign of Mars, known in Arabic as al-Qahir and associated with the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><p>A moulding of two parallel lines with loops between them runs along the upper façade, the earliest example of a feature that recurs often in later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> architecture. Inside, the vestibule has a shallow dome on pendentives, a feature more typical of Byzantine building, with the Sidi Zouk Tomb of an unidentified figure on the east side and a long vaulted chamber on the west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The gate itself bears no inscription, but one in floriated Kufic can be seen nearby to the east, on the wall around the northern minaret of the adjacent al-Hakim Mosque. Bab al-Futuh is one of three gates of the old city wall that remain, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bab-al-nasr">Bab al-Nasr</a> to the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bab-zuwayla">Bab Zuwayla</a> to the south. Vittore Carpaccio depicted it in his painting St. George and the Dragon of 1502.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bab al-Futuh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Badr al-Jamali afterwards rebuilt the state, and the gates of Bab Zuwayla, <b>Bab al-Futuh</b>, and Bab al-Nasr that he built still stand.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
